Six months of Jublia and the nail looks exactly the same. That is not a failure of willpower, and it is rarely a failure of the product itself. It is usually a mismatch between treatment and severity, which is precisely the gap most articles about the <strong>best toenail fungus treatment</strong> never close. They describe options. They do not tell you which one fits your nail.

So here is the direct answer first. The <strong>best toenail fungus treatment</strong> depends on how much of the nail is affected. Mild cases, caught early and limited to the nail tip, sometimes respond to OTC antifungals. Moderate to severe infections, the kind that have already spread toward the cuticle or thickened the whole nail, almost always need a prescription oral antifungal. Laser sits in a narrower lane: useful when pills are off the table, not a stronger option when they are not.

This comparison maps every option to a cure rate, a cost, and a severity stage, because that is the decision a frustrated person actually needs to make. For the full clinical picture on causes and diagnosis, the <a href='/blog/toenail-fungus/toenail-fungus'>complete toenail fungus guide</a> covers what gets you here. This post covers what gets you out.

