## How to Match Your Treatment to Your Infection Severity

Severity decides the treatment category. Not preference, not budget, not what an advert promised last spring.

Onychomycosis, the clinical term for a fungal nail infection, is staged by how much of the nail plate is involved and whether the infection has reached the matrix, the area under the cuticle where new nail grows. A mild case covers less than 25 percent of the nail with no matrix involvement. Moderate sits between 25 and 50 percent, often with some thickening. Severe means more than half the nail is affected, the matrix is compromised, or multiple nails are infected at once.

This matters because OTC and even some topical prescriptions physically cannot reach fungus once it sits deep in the nail bed near the matrix. The nail plate acts as a shield, and the thicker the infection, the less a surface treatment can do. That is not marketing spin, it is nail anatomy.

There is one complication worth naming directly: misdiagnosis. A meaningful share of thickened, discoloured nails are not fungal at all. They are trauma, psoriasis, or keratin buildup from years of polish. If your nail does not respond to an appropriate treatment course after a reasonable trial, it is worth checking <a href='/blog/toenail-fungus/toenail-fungus-vs-nail-psoriasis'>toenail fungus vs nail psoriasis</a>, because treating the wrong condition explains a lot of "nothing is working" stories.

A podiatrist confirms severity with a simple in-office look, sometimes backed by a fungal culture or KOH test. That confirmation is what makes the rest of this comparison useful instead of theoretical, because the <strong>best toenail fungus treatment</strong> for a mild case and the <strong>best toenail fungus treatment</strong> for a severe one are rarely the same product.

