## OTC Toenail Fungus Treatments: What They Can and Cannot Do

OTC antifungals are not a weaker version of the real thing. They are the right tool for a narrower job than most packaging admits.

Over-the-counter options, tolnaftate sprays, undecylenic acid solutions, and medicated nail lacquers, work on the surface of the nail. For genuinely mild infections caught early, that surface contact can be enough. The honest limitation is penetration: most topical formulas reach only the outer third of the nail plate. If the infection sits closer to the nail bed or has spread under more than a quarter of the nail, an OTC product is applying treatment to a layer the fungus has already moved past.

This is the gap nobody flags before purchase. "Clinically proven" language on packaging often refers to improved nail appearance, not mycological cure, the lab-confirmed elimination of the fungus itself. Those are different outcomes, and the difference is exactly why so many people describe months of OTC use with nothing changing.

OTC also occupies different territory than natural approaches. If tea tree oil or vinegar soaks are part of the conversation, those sit in <a href='/blog/toenail-fungus/toenail-fungus-home-remedies'>home remedies for toenail fungus</a> rather than the pharmaceutical OTC category covered here, and the evidence base for each is not interchangeable.

The realistic read: OTC is worth trying first only for genuinely mild, recently noticed infections. Past that threshold, it is a delay rather than a treatment, and delay lets the infection spread to more of the nail or to neighbouring nails.

