## Prescription Antifungal Pills: The Highest Cure Rates Explained

Oral terbinafine has the highest mycological cure rate of any single treatment in this comparison, clearing the infection in 70 to 80 percent of cases after a 12-week course, which is why it is widely considered the <strong>best toenail fungus treatment</strong> for anyone medically able to take it. No topical or laser option matches that number on its own.

Terbinafine works because it is systemic. Taken as a 250mg daily tablet, it circulates through the bloodstream and incorporates into the nail as it grows, reaching fungus at the nail bed that topical treatments cannot touch. This is the mechanical reason oral medication outperforms surface-applied options: it does not need to penetrate the nail from outside, it is already inside.

The trade-off is the conversation everyone has heard and few have had explained clearly. Terbinafine carries a liver monitoring requirement, baseline liver function tests before starting, with a follow-up check around four to six weeks in. Severe liver injury from terbinafine is rare, occurring in roughly 1 in 50,000 to 120,000 prescriptions, and most lab abnormalities that do appear are mild and resolve once treatment ends. That is a real risk worth monitoring, not a reason to avoid an effective medication outright. Itraconazole is the usual alternative for those who cannot take terbinafine, though it runs a lower cure rate, generally cited between 50 and 65 percent, and carries its own drug interaction profile.

Timeline matters here too. The 12-week course finishes before the nail looks normal. Most people notice the first clear band growing in around three to four months, with full visible clearance taking nine to twelve months as the healthy nail grows out completely. <a href="https://www.scripps.org/news_items/7235-which-toenail-fungus-treatment-is-best">Scripps Health guidance on oral antifungal treatment</a> reflects the same first-line positioning seen across podiatry practice.

