## Prescription Topical Treatments: When Pills Are Not an Option

Not everyone can take oral terbinafine, and that is exactly who prescription topicals exist for.

Liver disease, certain drug interactions, pregnancy, or simple intolerance to a previous oral antifungal all push the decision toward a topical route. Efinaconazole 10% solution, sold as Jublia, is the most studied option in this category. Applied once daily for 48 weeks, it carries no systemic side effects and no liver monitoring requirement, which is the entire appeal for someone who cannot safely take a pill.

Onychomycosis is the underlying fungal infection every treatment in this comparison targets, and Jublia's clinical trial data is worth stating plainly rather than rounding up. Complete cure, meaning a fully clear nail confirmed by negative lab testing, was achieved in roughly 17 to 18 percent of patients across two large phase 3 trials. Mycological cure alone, meaning the fungus itself was eliminated even if the nail had not fully regrown clear, reached around 55 percent. Those numbers sit well below oral terbinafine, and the honest reason is the same penetration limit that affects OTC: topical formulas, even prescription strength ones, still have to cross the nail plate from the outside.

Ciclopirox lacquer is the older topical option, with complete cure rates closer to 5 to 8 percent, generally considered the least effective prescription route available. Tavaborole sits in between. None of the topical options outperform oral medication in head-to-head cure rate data, which is worth knowing before paying out of pocket for a year of daily application expecting oral-level results.

