## Laser Treatment for Toenail Fungus: Costs, Cure Rates, and the Real Trade-Off

Laser does not outperform oral prescription antifungals in cure rate data, despite how it is frequently marketed. It earns its place for a different reason: zero systemic side effects and no drug interactions, which makes it most appropriate for people who cannot tolerate oral medication or want cosmetic improvement alongside another treatment.

The FDA clearance for nail laser therapy specifically covers "temporary increase in clear nail," not cure. Systematic reviews of mycological cure, the lab-confirmed elimination of fungus rather than just improved appearance, show rates in the 20 to 40 percent range, below what oral terbinafine achieves, though individual studies vary widely depending on laser type and protocol. Some podiatry practices report higher clinical improvement, 60 to 80 percent in some reporting, but that figure usually measures appearance rather than a confirmed fungal cure, and the distinction changes what the number actually promises a patient.

Cost is the other half of this trade-off. Laser sessions are typically billed per visit, are rarely covered by insurance since most plans classify nail laser as cosmetic, and several sessions are usually needed across months. For the full cost breakdown by session count and region, <a href='/blog/toenail-fungus/toenail-fungus-laser-treatment-cost'>laser treatment for toenail fungus cost</a> lays out current pricing in detail.

The realistic position: laser is a reasonable second-line choice for the right patient, not a premium upgrade over pills for someone who could otherwise take them safely.

