## Combination Therapy: Why Two Treatments Often Beat One

Combining oral terbinafine with a topical antifungal delivers the highest cure rate of any approach in this comparison, and it is not close. For anyone weighing the <strong>best toenail fungus treatment</strong> after one option has already failed, this is the data point that matters most: a randomised study comparing terbinafine alone against terbinafine plus topical ciclopirox found mycological cure rates of 88.2 percent for the combination versus 64.7 percent for terbinafine on its own.

The logic behind that gap is straightforward. Oral terbinafine clears fungus systemically through the nail bed, while the topical layer treats the nail plate surface directly, attacking the infection from two directions a single treatment cannot cover alone. For moderate to severe infections, particularly ones that have already failed a single-treatment attempt, this is increasingly what podiatrists recommend rather than escalating to a second oral course or jumping to laser.

It is not the answer for everyone. Combination therapy means committing to two routines, a daily pill for 12 weeks alongside months of topical application, and it carries the same liver monitoring requirement as terbinafine alone since the oral component has not changed. For someone who has already tried one OTC product, then a topical prescription, and seen no real movement, this is usually the next conversation worth having rather than trying a third single treatment in isolation.

