## How to Know If Your Treatment Is Actually Working

Most people judge a toenail fungus treatment by staring at the same spot every week and feeling nothing has changed. That is the wrong measure, and it is why so many people quit a treatment that was actually working.

The real marker is the base of the nail, not the tip. A clear band growing out from the cuticle means new, uninfected nail is replacing the old one, and that band should be visible within three to four months on an effective treatment. The tip of the nail, the oldest and most damaged part, is the last section to look normal, sometimes not until nine to twelve months in. Judging success by the tip alone means giving up right before the evidence would have shown up.

If no new clear growth appears by month four on oral terbinafine, or there is no improvement at all by month six on a topical, that is the point to revisit the plan with a podiatrist rather than waiting out the full course on faith. And once a course does clear the nail, the work is not done. Recurrence is common enough that <a href='/blog/toenail-fungus/prevent-toenail-fungus-returning'>prevent toenail fungus from coming back</a> is worth reading the same week treatment ends, not months later when it has already returned.

